# Working in this repository

This file is canonical; `CLAUDE.md` is a symbolic link to it. `main` is
protected: {% if host == 'gitlab' %}a change lands as a merge request whose pipeline passes.{% else %}a change lands as a pull request whose required checks are green.{% endif %}

```bash
uv sync --locked          # fails when the lockfile disagrees with pyproject
uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format --check .
uv run mypy .
uv run pytest
{% if host == 'gitlab' %}uv build                  # `build` is a pipeline job{% else %}uv build                  # `ci / build` is a required check{% endif %}
```

{% if host == 'gitlab' %}The pipeline runs each of these as a separate job. Pass them locally, then open the merge request.{% else %}CI runs each of these as a separate check. Pass them locally, then open the pull request.{% endif %}

## Where you are

- Starting something new: `/ask-matt`. Choosing a tool: `/plinth:arsenal`.
- Adding to what exists: read the issue, then `/implement #N`.
- Something is broken: read the failing check's log first, then `/mattpocock-skills:diagnosing-bugs`.
- Coming back: issues and their unticked acceptance criteria, {% if host == 'gitlab' %}open merge requests, the current branch{% else %}open pull requests, the current branch{% endif %} and uncommitted changes, before anything else. Claude Code's automatic memory stays on this machine, so a decision, an unverified item or a next step the next person needs {% if host == 'gitlab' %}goes into the issue or merge request, not only into memory.{% else %}goes into the issue or pull request, not only into memory.{% endif %}

## Always

- Before editing, inspect the branch, working-tree changes, worktree list and commits ahead of the intended base: `git fetch origin`, then `git status -sb`, `git worktree list`, `git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD`. Identify what belongs to this task. Those show the state of the checkout, not who else is using it: no command shows another session, so a clean checkout on `main` is not proof that it is yours alone. When it is not, or when it holds unrelated or unexplained work, leave it untouched and work in a uniquely named worktree branched from an explicitly verified base: `git worktree add --no-track -b <type>/<slug> .claude/worktrees/<slug> origin/main` (that path is ignored; one outside the repository works too). Never switch, reset, rebase or stash another task's checkout.
- {% if host == 'gitlab' %}The next piece of work is `glab issue list`.{% else %}The next piece of work is `gh issue list`.{% endif %} Acceptance criteria live in the issue, as lines a check or a reviewer can confirm; {% if host == 'gitlab' %}a merge request names, for each one it meets,{% else %}a pull request names, for each one it meets,{% endif %} the test that checks it, or why none does.
- A change in behaviour or a bug fix {% if host == 'gitlab' %}comes with the test that catches it, in the same merge request; say why when it does not.{% else %}comes with the test that catches it, in the same pull request; say why when it does not.{% endif %}
- `/implement` ends at a commit, {% if host == 'gitlab' %}so the merge-request rules below are not applied by it,{% else %}so the pull-request rules below are not applied by it,{% endif %} and a skill carrying its own example format does not override this repository's. Branch first, the way the first rule above says; {% if host == 'gitlab' %}`glab mr create` after. A merge request diff aims at 200 lines and stops at 400 (`diff-size`).{% else %}`gh pr create` after. A pull request diff aims at 200 lines and stops at 400 (`ci / diff-size`).{% endif %}
- {% if host == 'gitlab' %}The merge request title is `type(scope): summary` with one of the eleven standard types (`pr-title`).{% else %}The pull request title is `type(scope): summary` with one of the eleven standard types (`ci / pr-title`).{% endif %} Do not invent types; extra meaning goes in the scope. The description becomes the squash commit body when merged as `CONTRIBUTING.md` step 7 says and, when AI wrote or assisted, ends with `Assisted-by: <agent>:<model>` on its own last line. That is the only line that marks AI: remove a tool's default `Co-Authored-By` for the AI and its generated-with line, in commits too. A close, fix or resolve word before `#N` anywhere in the description closes that issue on merge, so word such a sentence another way.
- {% if host == 'gitlab' %}Merge only when the person says so for that merge request:{% else %}Merge only when the person says so for that pull request:{% endif %} a yes for one does not carry to the next, and "tidy up" is not a yes. A standing instruction the person gave for the session is the exception; name it when you merge on it. Merge with `CONTRIBUTING.md` step 7's command, {% if host == 'gitlab' %}never plain `glab mr merge`:{% else %}never plain `gh pr merge --squash`:{% endif %} it keeps the description as the commit and stops when a commit arrived after the one you checked.
- Before removing a worktree, run `git status --porcelain --ignored` in it and ask before anything listed is deleted: `git worktree remove` deletes ignored files, such as a local data file, without a word.
- {% if host == 'gitlab' %}Issues and merge requests follow{% else %}Issues and pull requests follow{% endif %} [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md#land-a-change) (the shape) and [docs/agents/issue-tracker.md](docs/agents/issue-tracker.md) (what a record has to carry). {% if host == 'gitlab' %}Read the templates in `.gitlab/` first; `glab --description` bypasses them.{% else %}Read the templates that actually apply first, your owner account's `.github` defaults included; `gh --body` bypasses them.{% endif %} These are this repository's conventions and yours to change; in someone else's repository, use theirs. Closing an issue writes its ending into the body: `## Outcome`, saying whether it was completed, cancelled, superseded or moved to a follow-up and what was verified, with each acceptance criterion that was met ticked and any left unticked named with why. {% if host == 'gitlab' %}`Closes #N` in a merge request description{% else %}`Closes #N` in a pull request description{% endif %} closes the issue on merge without anyone writing that, so whoever merges writes it.
- Changing behaviour: verify the boundaries and partial failures of the inputs and states it touches, record which paths you exercised and which you did not, and do not widen a few cases into a guarantee about all of them.
- Review findings: fix what is irreversible or reaches others before merging; a recoverable inconvenience is fixed if it is a few lines with a case, otherwise an issue; after two fix rounds, only the irreversible class is fixed and the rest becomes issues. Judge the class by what the diff does, not by the reviewer's severity label: a line of guidance that could say more exposes nothing, so it is never that class. When a finding repeats the last one's kind, rewrite once for the whole kind. Say on the thread what was not changed and why.
- Never ask for administration or workflow permission on the everyday token, and never answer a refusal by widening it: no re-login, {% if host == 'gitlab' %}no `glab auth login` with a wider token,{% else %}no `gh auth refresh` with more scopes,{% endif %} no other token in the session. {% if host == 'gitlab' %}A protected-branch or merge-check change is a person's: say which setting to change and why, then stop. The person makes it in the project's Settings.{% else %}A ruleset, required-check or code-scanning change is a person's: say which setting to change and why, then stop. The person types it through plinth's `scripts/with-admin-token.sh` or makes it in the repository's Settings. A push GitHub refuses because it changes `.github/workflows/` stops at the commit: name the branch and hand it to the person, who pushes it from a separate terminal with a credential that has the permission.{% endif %}
- Simplify within the agreed behaviour, the required checks and this file's rules; the checks a ticket agreed on are not negotiable.
- At four moments only (the start of a task, an important choice, a failure, the end or a resume) report in the five-item shape plinth's guidance defines (`/plinth:arsenal`), not every turn.
- Answer the person in the language they write in, summaries and reports included. {% if host == 'gitlab' %}Code, commits, merge requests and documents follow this repository's convention{% else %}Code, commits, pull requests and documents follow this repository's convention{% endif %}, a separate choice.
- What the required checks cannot undo, `.claude/settings.json` denies: force push, `rm -rf`, {% if host == 'gitlab' %}`glab repo delete`, reading or sourcing `.env` and `glab`'s token.{% else %}`gh repo delete`, reading or sourcing `.env` and `gh`'s token.{% endif %} The deny stops the Read tool, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, `grep`, `<`, `.` and `source`, and a `cat` or `grep` of `.env` inside `$(...)`, a subshell or a group; not `bash -c`, another reader inside `$(...)`, a search that does not name the file, or a script that opens it, which only the sandbox stops. A `.env` value is asked of the person, never loaded from the file.
- Write files with the Edit and Write tools. `.claude/settings.json` denies shell writes (a `cat` heredoc, `tee`, `sed -i`), so the agent's edits stay where the person can review them. A denied call is not a prompt and ran nothing, not even the commands chained before it: write the file with the edit tools and run those commands on their own. Never ask the person to approve it or to loosen the deny list.

